/-! # The x86 ELF probe corpus as problems (generated: do not edit)

Generated by `tools/verification-projects/x86_probe_problems.py gen` from `tools/verification-projects/problems/x86-probes.json`.

Each probe is a harness with one build, `plain` (its snapshot), on the hub's native x86-64
profile `Problems.nativeX86` (`probeProblem`). Every statement is a definition in the
published shape (`StatementProfile.haltsMayFail`, `StatementProfile.haltsWithinBudget`,
`StatementProfile.agrees`) or the extended one (`StatementProfile.haltsMayFailWith`, #3610),
with its `rfl` equation against `ProfileProblem.denote` (`denoteExt`, `PairProblem.denote`),
and an `Entry` carrying the catalogue's status. An extended statement implies its published
one (`<name>_base`). The proofs and refutations are in `Problems.X86Probes.Results`.

These are `ProfileProblem` statements over `nativeX86`, not `Problem.denote?` (`Shapes`)
forms. Their domain is the hub's x86 launch envelope: every entry with
`nativeX86.validEntry` (`= X86Launch.ValidEntry`, `nativeX86_validEntry`: the stack pointer
in its window below `TASK_SIZE` and 16-aligned, the mmap base in fixed bounds, the image
below the stack reservation) and every input with `nativeX86.validInput false`
(`nativeX86_validInput`: `X86Launch.ValidInput`, which fixes `hwcap = 0`, and `abort()` not
modelled). theorem.dev's riscv64 `Run.ValidInput` (an RV64GC(V) `hwcap`, Sv48,
`ValidEntryVa`) is a different domain.

A refuted entry's docstring names why (`refutedBy` in the catalogue). `modelCoverage`
refutations come from a call the model lacks (chaos) and refute every exit set, so they
say nothing about Linux's status; `shortWrite` refutations measure the model's short-write
semantics, not a Linux-observable difference.
-/

